{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304732,"nonce":"zN17xZUCET+eIOI0ATAU2pzTpbQ=","message":"Worked on branch `spec/temp-control` (forked from `main` at `fba467b`). Commits\n`8310dd3`, `89380ab`, `ec43711`, `748b267`, `df16de1`. `dotnet build -c Release`\nclean from a wiped `obj/`: 0 warnings, 0 errors. The branch carries no assembly.\n\n**NOTHING HERE HAS MET A BENCH.** Every claim below is source-verified or\nsuite-planned, not measured in game.\n\n## What I found before writing anything\n\nThe issue's grep still held — `grep -rn \"CompTempControl|targetTemperature|\nTargetTemperature|CompRottable|RotProgress\" Source/AutoRimmer/` returned nothing.\n`ThermalGovernor.cs` is CPU thermals for the advance's speed ladder and is\nunrelated. So the actuator gap was real and complete.\n\nBut **three factual claims in this issue are wrong**, and one of them would have\nproduced a defect if implemented as written.\n\n### 1. There is no def-side clamp, and the fields it names do not exist\n\nThe issue says \"the def-side `TemperatureControlProps` carry `minTemperature` /\n`maxTemperature`, which is the range the UI slider is clamped to\", and asks for a\nREFUSAL below it. Checked against the 1.6 tree:\n\n- **There is no slider.** `RimWorld/CompTempControl.CompGetGizmosExtra` yields\n  five `Command_Action` buttons: -10, -1, \"reset to 21\", +1, +10.\n- **The fields are `CompProperties_TempControl.minTargetTemperature` (-50) and\n  `.maxTargetTemperature` (50)**, not `minTemperature`/`maxTemperature`.\n- **Nothing in the tree reads either one.** Grepped unpiped over the whole\n  decompiled source: the only two hits are the declarations. They are dead.\n\nThe only clamp that exists is in `InterfaceChangeTargetTemperature`:\n`Mathf.Clamp(TargetTemperature, -273.15f, 1000f)`. So a player walks a cooler to\n-273 with the -10 button and the game does not stop them, and a verb that refused\nat -50 would **refuse something a player can do** — DESIGN's Action model broken\nin the other direction, the same class of error as bypassing a gate.\n\n**Resolved, not queued:** the def range is published on every row as\n`def_min_c`/`def_max_c` with `def_clamp_enforced: false`, a target outside it\nsets `outside_def_range: true` and produces a named advisory saying why it is not\na refusal, and the `bad-args` refusal is on the clamp that is real, citing the\nmember. DESIGN's decisions log carries it; `accept/261f2e9-temperature.py` checks\n3.13-3.17 assert the OPPOSITE of this issue's second acceptance bullet on\npurpose.\n\n### 2. The unpowered-cooler bullet resolves the same way\n\n`CompGetGizmosExtra` has no power clause either (and no faction clause — unlike\n`CompFlickable`, whose toggle really is gated on `parent.Faction ==\nFaction.OfPlayer`). So the bullet's second branch is taken: `powered:false` is\nREPORTED on every row, with an advisory citing\n`Building_Cooler.TickRare`'s `if (!compPowerTrader.PowerOn) return;`.\n\n### 3. The vent: `flick`, from the def rather than from judgement\n\nCore's `Buildings_Temperature.xml` gives `Vent` exactly one comp,\n`CompProperties_Flickable`, and NO `CompProperties_TempControl` — so\n`Building_Vent.compTempControl` is null even though `Building_Vent : Building_\nTempControl`, and `Building_Vent.TickRare` reads no target at all. `temp-set`\nrefuses it by name pointing at `flick`. Three signals (Temperature build\ncategory, \"temperature building\" label, temperature base class) all point the\nwrong way, which is why this was worth checking rather than assuming.\n\n## What shipped\n\n**`temp-control`** — the read. `{room:\u003cid\u003e} | {things|rect|cells|filter} | {}`\n(whole map = every player-faction building carrying the comp). Publishes def,\nposition, rotation, faction, kind, `target_c`, the def range, `powered`,\n`switch_on`, `broken_down`, `operating_at_high_power` (the game's OWN\n\"is it working\" field), `effective`, and `serves`/`exhaust`.\n\n**`temp-set`** — the actuator. Same addressing minus the whole-map default\n(refused: setting every cooler on the map at once is a mistake worth refusing).\nCelsius only — `Prefs.TemperatureMode` converts for DISPLAY, and a protocol whose\nunits depend on a user preference breaks when somebody clicks the F button.\nJournals one `action` row per call with `targets[*].{id,def,before_c,after_c}`.\n\n**The room a cooler SERVES is the game's own arithmetic, not \"the room it stands\nin\".** `Building_Cooler.TickRare` pushes into `Position +\nIntVec3.South.RotatedBy(Rotation)` and the waste heat into the north cell, and\nwraps the whole block in `if (!intVec2.Impassable(Map) \u0026\u0026 !intVec.Impassable(Map))`\n— a cooler walled in on either side draws idle power forever and moves no heat.\nBoth sides and both blocked flags are published. `Building_Heater.TickRare` uses\nits own room and gets its own citation.\n\n**Five silent no-op states get named advisories** rather than bare booleans:\noutdoor room (`ControlTemperatureTempChange` returns 0f), blocked cooler side,\nflicked off, broken down, unpowered. Each is a state where the target is accepted\nand never honoured — exactly the session-18 shape.\n\n## The half the issue understates\n\n`digest.temperature`, a registered predicate section. A room is watched when a\ncontroller serves it OR when it holds human-edible food. `ok` is deliberately\nnarrow: false only when a room a SWITCHED-ON controller serves is more than\n`tolerance_c` (2 C, ours, published) on the wrong side of its target. Switched-off\ncontrollers are excluded (`SwitchIsOn` is the player's own recorded intent);\nunpowered ones are not (a freezer whose net died is the emergency). Food warm in\nan uncontrolled room is COUNTED but not alarmed here — on a colony with no\nfreezer that alarm would be permanently on.\n\nCheap on session 19's axis, which is why it is registered: one walk of the real\n`allBuildingsColonist` list with a per-def memoised `HasComp`, one memoised food\nwalk, region-grid room lookups, field reads. **No `Room.Role` and no\n`Room.GetStat`** — so the section publishes no room ROLE, and a reader who wants\none calls `room \u003cid\u003e` and pays for it there.\n\n## The rot term\n\n`food_days` is **not** redefined. It is a shipped predicate target with suites\nasserting on it, and \"what the vanilla alert will do\" is a real question. What\nships beside it:\n\n- `resources.food_days_basis` — the disclaimer moved out of a source comment and\n  into the data. `Materials.cs` exists because a true warning in a comment did\n  not stop the code three lines below drawing a conclusion the agent could not\n  check (`54b0c9a`); this is that fix applied to the field the agent reads.\n- `resources.food_rot` — map-wide, `CompRottable.CompInspectStringExtra`'s own\n  three bands and 0.001/0.999 cutpoints, the `TicksUntilRotAtTemp` clock,\n  `spoiled_stacks` (what `ShouldCount`'s `IsNotFresh` silently drops from\n  `food_days`), and its own honest `days`.\n\n**`Materials.Of` is deliberately NOT used**, and its own header says why in its\nlast line: \"no predicate and no digest section reaches this file\". It pathfinds\nper stack per builder — session 19's named disqualifier — and it is per-def where\nfood is dozens of defs. The cheap half of the same correction ships instead, with\nthe bound STATED: `nutrition` is an UPPER bound (no reachability tested),\n`nutrition_in_stockpiles` is the LOWER one, `nutrition_forbidden` narrows the gap\nfor free.\n\nAll three of `ResourceCounter`'s membership clauses are reproduced, and the third\nis load-bearing: `CountAsResource` keeps CORPSES out, since corpse defs get\n`foodType = FoodTypeFlags.Corpse` and `OmnivoreHuman` (0x1F3F) HAS the 0x8 Corpse\nbit. Without it a battlefield lands in the larder figure and pins the alarm on\nforever. Excluded stacks are counted and published.\n\n## Found in audit, fixed here\n\nOne real behavioural bug and several hazards, from a read-only audit of every\ngetter these files touch against the decompiled source:\n\n- **`temp-set {room:X}` used to retarget every cooler that merely EXHAUSTS into\n  X** — i.e. the freezer's cooler, silently thawing the freezer. The read still\n  lists it; the actuator refuses it by name (`exhaust-only`) with the room it\n  actually serves. Regression guard at suite checks 1.28-1.30.\n- **`FoodRot`'s stack cap could hide the alarm.** The scan produces an aggregate\n  in `listerThings` insertion order and cannot order by importance before it\n  cuts, so a 600-stack cap meant `ok` could read TRUE because the stack about to\n  spoil was the 700th. Now a 5000-stack pathology ceiling that FAILS LOUD:\n  `ok:false` with `truncated:true` and a reason.\n- **The food walk ran twice per full digest** (`resources` and `temperature`).\n  Memoised per (map, tick) — exactly right, since game state only changes on a\n  tick.\n- `ThingWithComps.GetComp\u003cT\u003e()` throws IndexOutOfRange on an empty non-null comps\n  list (its `comps[0]` fast path); wrapped. `CompTempControl.Props` is a hard\n  cast that a modded comp can make throw; wrapped. `Faction.OfPlayer` `Log.Error`s\n  when null; switched to `OfPlayerSilentFail`. `RotProgressPct` can yield\n  Infinity on a modded `daysToRotStart: 0`; guarded.\n- **`Room.UsesOutdoorTemperature` is not a field read** — it walks every cell of\n  the room via `District.OpenRoofCountStopAt` on first read. Read once per\n  controller now, and the comment that called it a field read is corrected.\n- Fog: room rows are filtered through `WorldSafe.RoomHidden`.\n\n**Filed as its own issue, `d16a463`:** `IntVec3.GetRoom(map)` bottoms out in\n`RegionGrid.GetValidRegionAt`, whose first line is\n`map.regionAndRoomUpdater.TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms()` — so an observer read\ntaken in the same frame as a wall being built can flush a region rebuild, which\nrecreates `Room` objects (invalidating published `room_id`s) and reaches\n`autoBuildRoofAreaSetter.TryGenerateAreaFor` and `Building_Bed.ForPrisoners`,\nboth **scribed**. This is a pre-existing property of the whole observation\nsurface — `DigestVerb.ColonistSection`, `SpatialVerbs.RoomAt` and `PlaceVerbs` all\ntake the same route — so it is documented here and filed rather than fixed in one\nfile.\n\n## What is owed on a bench\n\n`accept/261f2e9-temperature.py` (+ `.md`), phases 0-5 in the sweep, 6 opt-in.\n`--selftest` passes 14 offline checks; `--dry-run` plans 156.\n\nOwed:\n1. **Phase 6 is the only proof that a cooler cools**, and it is the issue's own\n   third acceptance bullet. It needs the freezer fixture: a cooler in a wall with\n   BOTH sides passable, a sealed roofed room on the south side, and a power net\n   that has had one advance to form. The `.md` carries the recipe, including that\n   a solar generator stops at sunset.\n2. **The Vent-specific refusal text (3.7, 3.8) is skipped on a map with no Vent**\n   and reports a NOTE rather than passing a check it did not earn.\n3. **The exhaust-only regression guard (1.28-1.30) needs a cooler whose two sides\n   are different rooms.** Highest-value uncovered check on a bench without that\n   geometry.\n4. **The 2 C tolerance has never been measured against a real freezer's\n   oscillation.** It is published as `tolerance_c` and the suite pins its\n   expectation to the published value rather than to a literal, but the choice is\n   ours and untested.\n5. The suite does not clean up: it may leave a spawned `Heater` and 20\n   `MealSimple`, and phase 5 deliberately advances until food has rotted.","files":null}]}